# AI Meal Planner

A RAG-based meal planning assistant that generates personalized 7-day meal plans using a local LLM. Built to explore retrieval-augmented generation, prompt grounding, and automated evaluation.

What it does
Takes a user profile (goal, diet, allergies, calorie target, budget, available ingredients)
Retrieves the most relevant recipes from a local vector database
Passes only retrieved recipes to the LLM so it cannot invent meals outside the knowledge base
Returns a 7-day plan with a shopping list, macros, and cited sources

## Tech stack
Python, LangChain
Ollama (Llama 3)
Sentence Transformers (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) 
Streamlit 
Pytest
Docker + Docker Compose


## How to run with Docker (recommended)

Requirements: Docker Desktop

bash:
git clone https://github.com/gigaaaron/food-planner.git
cd food-planner
docker compose up --build

First run downloads the Llama 3 model (~4.7GB) and may take 10–15 minutes. Every run after that starts in seconds.

Open http://localhost:8501 once you see Starting Streamlit... in the logs.

To stop:
docker compose down


## Using real recipes (with source links and real nutrition facts)

`data/recipes.json` ships with a placeholder set. To replace it with real recipes
pulled from the web -- each with a working source link and real nutrition facts --
instead of hand-authored placeholders:

1. Get a free API key at https://spoonacular.com/food-api (free tier: 150 requests/day).
2. Add it to `.env`: `SPOONACULAR_API_KEY=your_key_here`
3. Fetch and re-index:

bash
python -m src.fetch_recipes
python -m src.ingest

(or, if running in Docker: `docker compose exec app python -m src.fetch_recipes` then
`docker compose exec app python -m src.ingest`, followed by `docker compose restart app`)

This overwrites `data/recipes.json` with real recipes across breakfast/lunch/dinner/snack,
each carrying its original source URL and Spoonacular-reported nutrition. The app shows each recipe's source link in the "Retrieved Recipes" tab, and under the generated plan for any recipe it actually used, so you can click through to the original page for full
cooking instructions.

Allergens are derived by scanning ingredient names for common allergen keywords (dairy, eggs, gluten, peanuts, tree nuts, soy, shellfish, fish, sesame) since Spoonacular doesn't tag every allergen category directly -- treat this as a starting point, not a guarantee,
and double check against the source recipe if you have a serious allergy.


## How to run locally without Docker

Requirements: Python 3.11+, Ollama installed

bash
git clone https://github.com/gigaaaron/food-planner.git
cd food-planner
python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate        # Windows
pip install -r requirements.txt
ollama pull llama3
python -m src.ingest

Then in two terminals:

bash
ollama serve
streamlit run streamlit_app.py
Project structure
food-planner/
├── src/
│   ├── ingest.py            # loads recipes into ChromaDB
│   ├── retrieval.py         # queries ChromaDB
│   ├── prompts.py           # prompt templates
│   ├── meal_generator.py    # RAG pipeline orchestration
│   └── evaluator.py         # eval metrics
├── ui/
│   ├── profile_form.py      # user input form
│   ├── results.py           # output display
│   └── styles.py            # UI helpers
├── utils/
│   └── feedback.py          # logs user feedback to CSV
├── tests/
│   ├── eval_dataset.json         # 30 hand-labeled eval questions
│   ├── test_retrieval.py         # retrieval and filter tests
│   └── test_meal_generation.py   # LLM output tests
├── data/
│   └── recipes.json         # 20 seed recipes
├── styles.css
├── Dockerfile
└── docker-compose.yml


## Evaluation results

Evaluated against 30 hand-labeled questions across 6 categories using keyword hit rate, allergen safety, context precision, and answer relevance.

Metric	Score
Overall score	65.8%
Pass rate (score >= 0.6)	70.0% (21/30)
Keyword hit rate	49.3%
Allergen safety	86.7%
Context precision	53.3%
Answer relevance	55.8%

By category:

Category	Score
Goal alignment	79.5%
Ingredient match	75.0%
Dietary constraint	72.5%
Retrieval relevance	70.7%
Calorie constraint	69.1%
Allergen safety (category)	35.1%

The low allergen safety category score is a known scoring artifact: the eval checks whether allergen words appear anywhere in the response, which flags cases where the model correctly states "this recipe contains no peanuts." Hard filter tests in pytest confirm allergens are correctly excluded at the retrieval layer. The weakest genuine metric is keyword hit rate (49.3%), which reflects Llama 3 running on CPU without GPU acceleration — a hosted model would score meaningfully higher without any pipeline changes.

Running the eval

Quick run (5 questions):

bash
python -m src.evaluator

Full run (30 questions, ~20 min): change the last line of src/evaluator.py from run_eval(max_questions=5) to run_eval() then run the same command. Results are saved to tests/eval_results.json.

Running tests

Fast tests, no LLM required:

bash
pytest tests/test_retrieval.py -v

Full test suite including LLM output checks:

bash
pytest tests/test_meal_generation.py -v